$DIR/main.p:22:11: p holds a view of s (&s.c.x, line 20), which was passed to reset(), which may free what it looks at or move it out (g.c = ..., line 13) at line 21, so p can no longer be used here
    p looks at memory that s refers to, without owning it: the view was made by
    &s.c.x at line 20. A view obtained through a counted reference held in a
    local variable may be used only while the variable keeps the reference the
    view was made from. A call that may free memory the view looks at, or move
    an owner out of it, through a pointer to the variable or to memory it owns
    ends the view as well. At line 21 s was passed to reset(), which may free
    what it looks at or move it out (g.c = ..., line 13), so the view may look
    at memory that was released, or at a value that now belongs to something
    else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use p before line 21, or make that call only after the last use of p
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)
